---
// Figure: a rebuild lock in Redis, against single-flight.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Choice from '../shared/Choice.astro';
import Readout from '../shared/Readout.astro';
import './stampedes.css';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="st-lock" title="One rebuilder for all processes" advance={1000} legend={['client', 'hit', 'miss', { kind: 'write', label: 'Write, including the lock' }]}
  caption="The same model, at 15 requests a second. With the lock, a process that misses sends SET … NX to Redis (a square); the one that gets OK rebuilds, and every other request waits and polls Redis every 150 ms (slowed down like everything else here) until the new copy appears.">
  <Choice slot="params" name="mode" label="When a process misses" value="lock"
    options={[{ value: 'singleflight', label: 'single-flight' }, { value: 'lock', label: 'rebuild lock in Redis' }]} />
  <Fragment slot="stats">
    <Readout name="queries" label="Database queries, latest expiry" kind="database" />
    <Readout name="slowest" label="Slowest response, latest expiry" kind="client" />
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>With single-flight, each expiry sends four queries, one per process. With the rebuild lock, each expiry sends exactly one: the first process to take the lock rebuilds, and every other request that missed, in any process, waits and polls Redis until the copy is stored. The cost appears in the slowest response: about 1.1 seconds with single-flight, about 1.3 to 1.5 seconds with the lock, because taking the lock and polling add round trips to Redis. In both cases the requests that arrive during the rebuild wait for it.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { mountHotKey } from './hotkey-scene.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="st-lock"]').forEach(root => mountHotKey(root));
</script>
